mod core;

// Zeroable

/// This is a marker interface (meaning it has no methods) that is usually automatically implemented on types by the compiler.
/// Types that implement this interface can be zero-initialized by variables with no initializers.
/// - Primitives, Pointers and Enums are always `Zeroable`.
/// - References, Functions and Interfaces are *NOT* `Zeroable`.
/// - Arrays are `Zeroable` if their inner type is `Zeroable`.
/// - Structs are `Zeroable` if all their fields are `Zeroable` (unless overwritten by a manually implementation, see below),
///   and if no field has the `required` attribute.
///
/// This interface shouldn't be implemented on types manually, unless absolutely necessary.
/// Where you can be sure, that a non `Zeroable` value will *never* be accessed before it is written.
/// One example of this is the `Option` type.
pub interface Zeroable {}
